###############################################################################
#  The two halves of this directory are stored differently, and git is TOLD
#  which is which rather than left to infer it.
#
#  The repository root sets `* text=auto` and this clone runs core.autocrlf, so
#  without this file git decides per-file whether to rewrite line endings by
#  sniffing for NUL bytes. That heuristic gets the right answer for the objects
#  only by accident, and a corrupted oracle is worse than a missing one: it
#  fails while looking authoritative.
#
#  OBJECTS are byte-verbatim vendor artifacts -- the bytes every corpus
#  comparison is made against. Any transformation destroys them, so they stay
#  binary. They carry NULs and would probably be classified binary anyway;
#  "probably" is not a basis for the file the whole corpus is measured against.
#
#  SOURCES are committed as ordinary Windows text, which is what lets one file
#  feed both the unit tests and `CassoCli merlin`. `eol=crlf` makes that a
#  property of the repository rather than of whoever cloned it: a unit test
#  asserts the CRLF is there, and without this line that assertion would be
#  measuring the developer's core.autocrlf setting instead.
#
#  `binary` is a macro for `-diff -merge -text`, so restoring text also takes
#  restoring diff and merge. Setting `text` alone leaves a source file
#  undiffable, which is the opposite of the point.
###############################################################################
* binary

*.S            text eol=crlf diff merge
T.*            text eol=crlf diff merge

README.md      text=auto diff merge
LICENSE        text=auto diff merge
.gitattributes text=auto diff merge
